  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nWe propose an evolutionary optimization strategy to escape local minima and explore the solution space more thoroughly. This approach is suitable for non-convex problems and can potentially find better minima than gradient descent. The method uses mutation and crossover operations while maintaining the integral constraint through careful scaling and clipping.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nDefine h as a step function with n_points samples from [0,1], satisfying \u2211h = n_points / 2. The objective is to minimize C\u2085 = max_lag (correlation(h, 1-h)[lag]) * dx, where dx = 2.0 / n_points. The evolutionary strategy maintains populations of h vectors, each respecting the constraints.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\na) Initialize population:\n- If initial_h_values exists, create 50 variants by adding small random noise (\u00b10.05) to h[i], clipping to [0,1], and scaling to maintain sum.\n- Else, initialize 50 random h vectors with sum n_points / 2.\n\nb) Mutation operation:\n- For each candidate, add noise with std=0.02 to 20% of elements.\n- Clip mutated values to [0,1].\n- Scale h *= (n_points / 2) / np.sum(h).\n\nc) Crossover operation:\n- Combine two parents by taking the average of their values.\n- Ensure sum constraint by scaling.\n\nd) Evaluate each candidate by computing C\u2085 using np.correlate.\n\ne) Selection: Keep top 20% of population based on lowest C\u2085.\n\nf) Replacement: Replace bottom 20% with new mutants and crossovers.\n\ng) Repeat until time budget is exhausted.\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\nStart with population size=50, mutation rate=0.2, crossover rate=0.5. If no improvement in 100 generations, increase mutation rate to 0.3.\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nEach evaluation of 100 candidates takes ~5s. With 600s, expect ~1200 evaluations (24 generations). Mutation and crossover operations are O(n_points), feasible within budget.\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nEnsure each h maintains 0 <= h[i] <= 1 and \u2211h = n_points / 2. Validate C\u2085 calculation using the provided evaluator. Return best h found, its C\u2085, and n_points.",
